WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.321
This is a Unitree G1 humanoid, in full physics simulation,

00:00:04.321 --> 00:00:07.336
walking through rooms it has never seen before.

00:00:07.686 --> 00:00:10.337
It ducks under a scaffold brace, then pulls its

00:00:10.337 --> 00:00:12.254
arms in to slip between two carts.

00:00:12.604 --> 00:00:14.448
It weaves through a slalom of crates,

00:00:14.448 --> 00:00:16.789
and finds its way across a cluttered warehouse.

00:00:17.139 --> 00:00:20.852
Every one of these movements was planned by a network that learned

00:00:20.852 --> 00:00:24.172
whole-body navigation, in large part, from imagined scenes.

00:00:26.167 --> 00:00:28.851
This video explains how. We call it Two Flows.

00:00:29.201 --> 00:00:31.861
We will go step by step: the problem, the idea,

00:00:31.861 --> 00:00:35.088
the one tool behind it, the scene generator, the teacher,

00:00:35.088 --> 00:00:38.597
the arena, and the student that brings it toward a real robot.

00:00:40.401 --> 00:00:45.083
Most robots navigate on a flat map: a floor plan where every cell is either free

00:00:45.083 --> 00:00:48.536
or blocked, and a path that threads through the free cells.

00:00:48.886 --> 00:00:52.877
But a humanoid is not a disk on a floor plan. It can duck under a beam,

00:00:53.227 --> 00:00:55.285
or pull its arms in to slip through a gap

00:00:55.285 --> 00:00:56.942
that its swinging arms would hit.

00:00:57.292 --> 00:00:59.394
So the question is not only where to walk,

00:00:59.394 --> 00:01:01.646
but how the whole body should move, and when.

00:01:01.996 --> 00:01:05.767
Learning that takes data: thousands of scenes, each paired with a

00:01:05.767 --> 00:01:09.944
whole-body motion that gets through it. Collecting those pairs on a real

00:01:09.944 --> 00:01:13.193
robot, or designing them by hand, simply does not scale.

00:01:14.801 --> 00:01:18.325
Our idea flips the problem around. Motion is plentiful:

00:01:18.325 --> 00:01:22.939
human motion capture, text-to-motion models, and the robot's own skills.

00:01:23.289 --> 00:01:26.243
So we start from a motion, like this ducking walk,

00:01:26.243 --> 00:01:29.848
and imagine the obstacles that would make exactly this motion

00:01:29.848 --> 00:01:33.039
necessary. This is called learning from hallucination.

00:01:33.389 --> 00:01:37.590
Necessary is the key word. Next to the real motion, we keep what the body

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:40.814
would have done otherwise; here, simply walking upright.

00:01:41.164 --> 00:01:45.304
A beam that both versions clear explains nothing. A beam so low that

00:01:45.304 --> 00:01:49.567
even the duck hits it is impossible. Only a beam that the upright walk

00:01:49.567 --> 00:01:52.307
hits, and the duck clears, explains the duck.

00:01:52.657 --> 00:01:56.111
Every imagined scene is checked exactly against the body's

00:01:56.111 --> 00:01:58.970
geometry, so each pair comes with a certificate:

00:01:58.970 --> 00:02:02.840
this motion solves this scene, and the easy alternative does not.

00:02:04.435 --> 00:02:07.789
Put together, this gives the two flows at the heart of the project.

00:02:08.139 --> 00:02:11.954
Flow one, SceneFlow, goes from motion to scene. Given a motion,

00:02:11.954 --> 00:02:16.010
it generates many different scenes that make that motion necessary.

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:20.113
Flow two, the flow planner, goes the other way. Given a scene and a

00:02:20.113 --> 00:02:23.305
goal, it generates the whole-body motion that gets there.

00:02:23.655 --> 00:02:28.161
Flow one manufactures the training data for flow two. That planner is our

00:02:28.161 --> 00:02:32.234
teacher. In simulation, it gets a perfect map of its surroundings.

00:02:32.584 --> 00:02:37.059
Its plans are carried out by SONIC, a frozen whole-body motion tracker that

00:02:37.059 --> 00:02:40.699
turns planned motion into joint commands, under full physics.

00:02:41.049 --> 00:02:44.635
A student planner then learns the same job from the robot's

00:02:44.635 --> 00:02:47.248
own lidar, the sensor a real robot carries.

00:02:47.598 --> 00:02:50.720
And to measure all of this honestly, we built an arena of

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:53.349
scenes that none of these models was trained on.

00:02:54.935 --> 00:02:57.637
Both halves of our system are built on one tool,

00:02:57.637 --> 00:03:01.015
called flow matching. Here is the whole idea in one picture.

00:03:01.365 --> 00:03:05.158
Start with simple noise: a cloud of random points. Next to it,

00:03:05.158 --> 00:03:10.359
a set of real examples. In this toy, the examples are points on a robot's silhouette.

00:03:10.709 --> 00:03:13.533
During training, we pair each noise point with a real

00:03:13.533 --> 00:03:16.197
example, and connect the two with a straight line.

00:03:16.547 --> 00:03:21.269
We pick a random time t along that line. The network sees the in-between point,

00:03:21.269 --> 00:03:24.916
and must predict the velocity that carries it to the example.

00:03:25.266 --> 00:03:29.838
Averaged over many pairs, those predictions form a smooth velocity field.

00:03:29.838 --> 00:03:33.971
Watch how the field reshapes itself as time runs from zero to one.

00:03:34.321 --> 00:03:37.797
To generate, we drop fresh noise into the field and follow

00:03:37.797 --> 00:03:40.914
the arrows, in a few dozen small steps. That is just

00:03:40.914 --> 00:03:43.431
solving an ordinary differential equation.

00:03:43.781 --> 00:03:46.884
Now add a condition. Ask for a ducking pose instead,

00:03:46.884 --> 00:03:49.569
and the very same noise flows somewhere else.

00:03:49.919 --> 00:03:52.261
That is all SceneFlow and our planner are:

00:03:52.261 --> 00:03:55.717
the same recipe, with different data and different conditions.

00:03:57.302 --> 00:04:01.231
Before SceneFlow can imagine scenes, it needs examples of good

00:04:01.231 --> 00:04:04.399
ones. These come from a slower, exact data engine.

00:04:04.749 --> 00:04:08.112
It starts from groups of motions that share the same past,

00:04:08.112 --> 00:04:11.532
but continue differently: duck, side-step, or keep walking.

00:04:11.532 --> 00:04:16.751
Each future is a target, and its siblings are the alternatives that a scene must rule out.

00:04:17.101 --> 00:04:19.846
On an alternative's body, the engine finds critical

00:04:19.846 --> 00:04:21.891
points that the target stays clear of.

00:04:22.241 --> 00:04:24.016
From such a point it grows a box,

00:04:24.016 --> 00:04:26.489
face by face, until it would touch the target.

00:04:26.839 --> 00:04:30.704
Then an exact check with forty-five body capsules keeps only scenes

00:04:30.704 --> 00:04:33.532
that the target clears, and the alternative hits.

00:04:33.882 --> 00:04:37.260
From human motion capture, text-to-motion clips and SONIC

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:40.638
skills, this produced over four hundred thousand verified

00:04:40.638 --> 00:04:43.543
scenes, for thirty-three thousand target motions.

00:04:45.136 --> 00:04:48.866
SceneFlow itself is a flow-matching model over sets of boxes.

00:04:49.216 --> 00:04:52.913
A scene is up to eight box slots. Each slot is twelve numbers:

00:04:52.913 --> 00:04:56.610
whether it exists, what kind of obstacle it is, where it sits,

00:04:56.610 --> 00:05:00.962
how it is turned, how large it is, and the heights of its bottom and top.

00:05:01.312 --> 00:05:06.071
The condition is the motion: twenty-eight snapshots spanning almost three seconds,

00:05:06.071 --> 00:05:10.598
each with thirty body points. Optional knobs, like the clearance margin or the

00:05:10.598 --> 00:05:13.615
number of obstacles, can steer it, or be left blank.

00:05:13.965 --> 00:05:19.172
A transformer encodes the motion. A second one turns noisy slots into a velocity,

00:05:19.172 --> 00:05:24.379
letting the slots attend to each other and to the motion. It has thirteen million

00:05:24.379 --> 00:05:27.658
parameters, and trains in under an hour on one GPU.

00:05:28.008 --> 00:05:30.783
To sample, we follow the flow for forty steps.

00:05:30.783 --> 00:05:34.343
In the second half, two gentle corrections nudge the boxes:

00:05:34.343 --> 00:05:37.480
stay clear of the motion, and block the alternative.

00:05:37.830 --> 00:05:40.616
Finally, each box is snapped snug against the body,

00:05:40.616 --> 00:05:43.403
and the exact checker accepts or rejects the scene.

00:05:45.003 --> 00:05:48.394
Here is SceneFlow on a ducking motion it never saw in training.

00:05:48.744 --> 00:05:51.829
Eight noisy boxes drift, settle, and lock into a scene:

00:05:51.829 --> 00:05:55.642
beams low enough to force the duck, and a wall that shapes the path.

00:05:55.992 --> 00:05:58.685
After the snap, the duck clears every box by a few

00:05:58.685 --> 00:06:01.594
centimetres, while walking upright would hit the beam.

00:06:01.944 --> 00:06:05.483
Sample again, and you get a different scene for the same motion.

00:06:05.483 --> 00:06:08.968
Here are eight: six pass the exact check, and two are rejected.

00:06:09.318 --> 00:06:13.166
Each kind of motion gets its own kind of scene: beams for ducks,

00:06:13.166 --> 00:06:16.955
close walls for sideways shuffles, low covers for crouch-walks.

00:06:17.305 --> 00:06:20.908
Across six hundred held-out motions, two out of three samples

00:06:20.908 --> 00:06:24.334
pass the full exact check. The slow engine, which sees the

00:06:24.334 --> 00:06:27.110
alternatives directly, passes four out of five.

00:06:28.703 --> 00:06:32.004
One more piece connects the two flows. We call it CycleFlow.

00:06:32.354 --> 00:06:36.636
For each generated scene, we ask a planner how much better it predicts the real

00:06:36.636 --> 00:06:39.835
motion when it sees the scene, compared with an empty room.

00:06:40.185 --> 00:06:43.574
That gap measures how much the scene says about the motion.

00:06:43.574 --> 00:06:47.651
For every motion, we keep the most informative of four verified scenes.

00:06:48.001 --> 00:06:52.273
Teachers trained on these cycle-filtered scenes reached more goals than

00:06:52.273 --> 00:06:56.605
teachers trained on randomly chosen verified scenes: thirty-seven versus

00:06:56.605 --> 00:07:00.396
thirty-three out of fifty-six, without guidance. With guidance,

00:07:00.396 --> 00:07:03.765
the gain shrinks to two goals, which is not significant.

00:07:05.370 --> 00:07:07.545
Now the second flow: the teacher planner.

00:07:07.895 --> 00:07:11.483
It looks at a privileged height map, three metres by one and a

00:07:11.483 --> 00:07:14.840
half, centred on the robot. One layer holds the heights of

00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:18.197
obstacles on the ground, one holds waist-height overhangs,

00:07:18.197 --> 00:07:21.091
and one holds how low the overhead obstacles hang.

00:07:21.441 --> 00:07:25.212
It also gets the last few frames of its own motion, and a local goal.

00:07:25.562 --> 00:07:29.071
Out comes one second of whole-body motion: body height,

00:07:29.071 --> 00:07:32.005
tilt, speed, and all twenty-nine joint angles,

00:07:32.005 --> 00:07:36.406
frame by frame. Here, the plan lowers the body to pass under a brace.

00:07:36.756 --> 00:07:41.188
It is trained with the same flow-matching recipe, on sixty-eight thousand

00:07:41.188 --> 00:07:45.438
windows of ordinary navigation motion, plus fifteen thousand SceneFlow

00:07:45.438 --> 00:07:49.142
necessity windows, which are sampled almost half of the time.

00:07:49.492 --> 00:07:52.293
Training takes about half an hour on one GPU,

00:07:52.293 --> 00:07:54.970
and every result uses three training seeds.

00:07:56.570 --> 00:07:59.809
Every 0.16 seconds, the teacher makes a new plan.

00:07:59.809 --> 00:08:03.576
Here is one planning step in the slalom, seen from above.

00:08:03.926 --> 00:08:06.876
First, a route to the goal, computed on the true map,

00:08:06.876 --> 00:08:09.603
that prefers to keep its distance from obstacles.

00:08:09.953 --> 00:08:13.671
A local goal is placed a short distance ahead, steering toward the route.

00:08:14.021 --> 00:08:17.235
The flow planner then samples sixteen candidate motions in

00:08:17.235 --> 00:08:19.729
parallel, each starting from different noise.

00:08:20.079 --> 00:08:23.683
While they form, a guidance term pushes every body part away from

00:08:23.683 --> 00:08:26.622
the obstacles, which are padded by eight centimetres.

00:08:26.972 --> 00:08:30.184
Each candidate is checked against the padded obstacles.

00:08:30.184 --> 00:08:33.337
Here, eleven of the sixteen keep the whole body clear.

00:08:33.337 --> 00:08:37.717
Among those, the planner picks the one that ends closest to the local goal.

00:08:38.067 --> 00:08:40.578
Only the first sixth of the plan is executed.

00:08:40.578 --> 00:08:43.926
Then the next plan starts from wherever the robot really is.

00:08:44.276 --> 00:08:48.602
Here is the whole run. Wherever the route's clearance drops below forty-five

00:08:48.602 --> 00:08:52.586
centimetres, a tight mode slows the robot down and brings its arms in.

00:08:54.170 --> 00:08:56.725
Here is a planning step in 3-D. On the left,

00:08:56.725 --> 00:09:00.267
the network alone: none of its sixteen candidates keeps clear

00:09:00.267 --> 00:09:03.577
of the beam. On the right, with guidance, all sixteen do.

00:09:03.927 --> 00:09:08.455
The chosen plan goes to SONIC, a general whole-body motion tracker that was

00:09:08.455 --> 00:09:12.501
trained separately. We keep it frozen, and it never sees the scene.

00:09:12.851 --> 00:09:16.369
SONIC turns the plan into joint targets fifty times a second,

00:09:16.369 --> 00:09:20.003
and Isaac Lab simulates the physics two hundred times a second.

00:09:20.353 --> 00:09:24.746
Because the teacher replans from the measured state every 0.16 seconds,

00:09:24.746 --> 00:09:28.273
the loop absorbs slips and tracking errors along the way.

00:09:29.870 --> 00:09:32.501
Put together, the teacher handles scenes like these.

00:09:32.851 --> 00:09:36.062
An office corridor with signs and door headers at head height.

00:09:36.412 --> 00:09:39.285
A validation scene that SceneFlow generated from a

00:09:39.285 --> 00:09:41.353
motion the teacher never trained on.

00:09:41.703 --> 00:09:44.769
A parking lot where the only way through is a fifty-six

00:09:44.769 --> 00:09:47.055
centimetre slot between a car and a wall.

00:09:47.405 --> 00:09:50.884
And the duck-then-tuck drill from the very beginning, seen from the front.

00:09:52.470 --> 00:09:56.689
To know whether any of this generalizes, we needed a test the models

00:09:56.689 --> 00:09:59.916
never trained on. So we built the G1 Traverse Arena.

00:10:00.266 --> 00:10:04.431
It has seventeen families of scenes: offices, warehouses, lobbies,

00:10:04.431 --> 00:10:08.154
parks, parking lots, debris fields, and drills for ducking,

00:10:08.154 --> 00:10:13.896
squeezing, slaloms and dead ends. Four families are sealed, and kept out of every decision.

00:10:14.246 --> 00:10:17.225
Its generator is separate from our training data engine,

00:10:17.225 --> 00:10:19.405
and every scene ships with a certificate.

00:10:19.755 --> 00:10:23.606
In the whole-body tier, a flat route with a fixed posture must

00:10:23.606 --> 00:10:27.519
fail, a posture-aware route must succeed, and a real G1 motion,

00:10:27.519 --> 00:10:30.438
executed by SONIC, must fit through every gate.

00:10:30.788 --> 00:10:34.795
We tune on validation scenes that SceneFlow makes from held-out

00:10:34.795 --> 00:10:37.848
motions, check every change on arena dev scenes,

00:10:37.848 --> 00:10:41.282
and read the sealed test only once per adopted recipe.

00:10:41.632 --> 00:10:45.054
We audit ourselves, too. When we found that the robot could

00:10:45.054 --> 00:10:48.071
simply walk around the outside of open arena scenes,

00:10:48.071 --> 00:10:51.087
we closed them with walls, and reset every baseline.

00:10:52.704 --> 00:10:54.532
Here is where the teacher stands today.

00:10:54.882 --> 00:10:59.404
On the validation scenes, goals rose from thirty-three to forty-nine out of

00:10:59.404 --> 00:11:03.684
seventy-one, mostly from two fixes: a route that covers the whole task,

00:11:03.684 --> 00:11:07.121
and slowing down with the arms tucked in narrow passages.

00:11:07.471 --> 00:11:10.877
On the closed arena dev scenes, the current teacher reaches

00:11:10.877 --> 00:11:13.649
sixty-seven percent of its goals, with no falls.

00:11:13.999 --> 00:11:17.864
The whole-body tier is much harder: thirteen out of a hundred and

00:11:17.864 --> 00:11:22.383
twenty-nine. Ducks under beams always work here. Slots barely wider than the

00:11:22.383 --> 00:11:25.832
robot, and gaps that need walking sideways, mostly do not.

00:11:26.183 --> 00:11:29.950
Two failures show where the limits are. Asked to duck under a

00:11:29.950 --> 00:11:33.717
sign at one point zero six metres, SONIC lowers the body only

00:11:33.717 --> 00:11:36.002
so far, and the torso clips the sign.

00:11:36.352 --> 00:11:39.441
And walking straight at a torso-height counter fails,

00:11:39.441 --> 00:11:43.229
while turning sideways gets through. That sideways run comes from

00:11:43.229 --> 00:11:46.318
an experimental variant that we have not adopted yet.

00:11:47.904 --> 00:11:51.233
A real robot has no perfect map. So the student planner

00:11:51.233 --> 00:11:54.200
builds its own, from the G1's head-mounted lidar.

00:11:54.550 --> 00:11:57.942
We simulate that lidar ray by ray. Each ray returns the

00:11:57.942 --> 00:12:00.965
first surface it meets: the floor, the side of an

00:12:00.965 --> 00:12:03.864
obstacle, or the underside of an overhead beam.

00:12:04.214 --> 00:12:07.663
Returns are written into a memory grid. The student reads

00:12:07.663 --> 00:12:10.810
the same three-metre map as the teacher, except that

00:12:10.810 --> 00:12:13.473
anything it has not seen yet counts as free.

00:12:13.823 --> 00:12:17.079
The student keeps the teacher's network and training targets,

00:12:17.079 --> 00:12:21.082
but learns them from these lidar maps, replayed along the training motions.

00:12:21.432 --> 00:12:25.204
Occlusion matters. Here, the second beam hides behind the first.

00:12:25.204 --> 00:12:29.095
The plain student walks into it. Enlarging every beam it has seen,

00:12:29.095 --> 00:12:33.280
by up to sixty centimetres, makes it cautious, and it ducks under both.

00:12:33.630 --> 00:12:37.251
On a fresh panel of two hundred and fifty-two tasks, read once,

00:12:37.251 --> 00:12:41.448
the student with its own lidar route reached two hundred and seven goals.

00:12:41.448 --> 00:12:45.070
The teacher, with a perfect map, reached two hundred and eight.

00:12:46.671 --> 00:12:50.286
The last step is the real robot, and we have built the whole path to it.

00:12:50.636 --> 00:12:54.471
On the G1, a node reads the robot's own Mid-360 point cloud,

00:12:54.471 --> 00:12:58.114
removes the robot's own body from it, corrects for motion

00:12:58.114 --> 00:13:00.990
during each sweep, and fills the same memory.

00:13:01.340 --> 00:13:06.682
A fused GPU version of the planner returns a plan in under two tenths of a second,

00:13:06.682 --> 00:13:10.656
two to three times faster than before, with the same choices.

00:13:10.656 --> 00:13:15.477
SONIC runs in C++ on the robot, and a gamepad can take over at any moment.

00:13:15.827 --> 00:13:19.743
We rehearse the full stack in MuJoCo, with the real lidar pattern,

00:13:19.743 --> 00:13:23.541
odometry noise and delays. It reaches eighty-two of ninety-eight

00:13:23.541 --> 00:13:26.686
goals, close to the eighty-three it reaches in Isaac.

00:13:27.036 --> 00:13:32.233
The rehearsal also showed what matters most: an uncalibrated three-degree tilt of

00:13:32.233 --> 00:13:36.019
the lidar drops it to fifteen. So calibration is mandatory.

00:13:36.369 --> 00:13:40.344
Nothing has run on the physical robot yet. The next steps are a

00:13:40.344 --> 00:13:44.194
staged hardware ladder: standing perception, then open floor,

00:13:44.194 --> 00:13:46.907
then ground obstacles, and only then beams.

00:13:48.505 --> 00:13:51.079
To close, here are the principles behind this work.

00:13:51.429 --> 00:13:54.092
Generate necessity, not clutter. Every training

00:13:54.092 --> 00:13:56.302
scene exists because a motion needs it.

00:13:56.652 --> 00:13:59.263
Certify everything. Every training pair and

00:13:59.263 --> 00:14:01.511
every arena scene is checked exactly.

00:14:01.861 --> 00:14:05.390
Keep the test honest. Tune on learned validation scenes,

00:14:05.390 --> 00:14:09.234
test once on a sealed arena, and keep every new idea switched

00:14:09.234 --> 00:14:12.322
off by default until it wins a paired comparison.

00:14:12.672 --> 00:14:16.145
What comes next: reading the sealed test, teaching the planner to

00:14:16.145 --> 00:14:19.510
slip sideways through the tightest gaps, putting the student on

00:14:19.510 --> 00:14:21.807
the arena, and the first steps on hardware.

00:14:23.405 --> 00:14:26.363
Two flows. One imagines the world a motion needs.

00:14:26.363 --> 00:14:30.407
The other finds the motion a world demands. Thank you for watching.

